From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Yates <jonjryjo@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: stream: Require exclusive zero-copy use

The zero-copy producer/consumer cursors and mapped frame pools belong to
the stream device rather than to an open file. Allowing multiple open file
descriptions while zero-copy mode is active lets unrelated callers submit,
reap, or repost each other's slots and corrupt the ownership protocol.

Reject enabling zero-copy when more than one user already has the device
open, and reject new opens after zero-copy has been enabled. Regular stream
read/write mode retains its existing multi-open behavior.

Signed-off-by: Jonathan Yates <jonjryjo@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c | 10 ++++++++++
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
index d54a0cd15563..1749b3017648 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
@@ -929,6 +929,10 @@ static int tbstream_dev_zc_enable(struct tbstream_dev *sdev)
 		ret = -ENXIO;
 		goto out_unlock;
 	}
+	if (sdev->users != 1) {
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+		goto out_unlock;
+	}
 	if (sdev->zc)
 		goto out_unlock;
 
@@ -1220,6 +1224,12 @@ static int tbstream_dev_fops_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *file)
 		}
 	}
 
+	/* Zero-copy pool ownership is device-wide and must stay exclusive. */
+	if (sdev->zc) {
+		ret = -EBUSY;
+		goto err_unlock;
+	}
+
 	/* Only on first open we allocate rings and enable paths */
 	if (!sdev->users++) {
 		ret = tbstream_dev_start(sdev);
-- 
